      "task_ids": [
        "a",
        "b"
      ],
      "solution": "a:x approx6.24 has three significant figures. Captured sqrt6.24 main value2.498 -> three significant figures2.50, not2.5 (two figures) or2.498 (four). b:x approx71 has two significant figures. Captured sqrt71 main8.426 -> two significant figures8.4. Leading zeros are not counted. These are the source hint's reporting conventions, not guaranteed enclosures ofsqrtx for an unbounded unspecified approximation."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "816",
      "source_task_count": 2,
      "task_ids": [
        "a",
        "b"
      ],
      "solution": "Selected reserve-digit table-first method for three-significant-figure inputs: a:31.4=3.14*10 -> square TableIII row3.1/main4=9.860 *100=986.0;27.1=2.71*10 -> row2.7/main1=7.344 *100=734.4. Actual table sum986.0+734.4=1720.4. Round this derived radicand to four significant figures1720=17.20*100. IV row17/main2=4.147 gives41.47; final three-significant-figure report41.5. For b:43.7=4.37*10 -> III row4.3/main7=19.10 *100=1910;21.6=2.16*10 -> row2.1/main6=4.666 *100=466.6. Actual table difference1910-466.6=1443.4. Four-significant-figure radicand1443=14.43*100; IV row14/main4=3.795 plus correction3=4 thousandths ->3.799, scale *10=37.99; final three-significant-figure report38.0. Preserve trailing zero. Exact decimal-surrogate squares, solely as a separate reference, are985.96,734.41,1909.69,466.56, giving1720.04 and1443.13 and exact-surrogate roots41.4733649... and37.9885509.... These are not substituted for the table sums/differences and are not actual true values of the approximate data. No last-digit accuracy guarantee is asserted."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "817",
      "source_task_count": 2,
      "task_ids": [
        "a",
        "b"
      ],
      "solution": "a:(x-5)^2 is positive except atx=5, where the product is0. Away from5 it does not change sign; thus the strict positive product requiresx-2>0 andx!=5. Full solution(2,5) union(5,infinity). x=2 and5 are excluded. b:(y+0.3)^2 is positive except aty=-0.3. Thus a strict negative product requiresy+0.2<0 andy!=-0.3. Full solution(-infinity,-0.3) union(-0.3,-0.2). Both zeros are excluded; squaring a factor removes its sign, not its zero."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "818",
      "source_task_count": 4,
      "task_ids": [
        "a",
        "b",
        "c",
        "d"
      ],
      "solution": "Real domains: a:1/sqrt(z-5) requiresz-5>=0 andsqrt(z-5)!=0, hencez>5, domain(5,infinity). b:1/(sqrtz+5) requiresz>=0; denominator>=5 is never0, domain[0,infinity). c:1/(sqrtz-5) requiresz>=0 andsqrtz!=5, hencez!=25, domain[0,25) union(25,infinity). d:sqrtz/(z-5) requiresz>=0 andz!=5, domain[0,5) union(5,infinity). Atz=0 the latter three expressions are defined; the location of the radical changes which zero must be excluded."
    },
    {
      "source_number": "819",
      "source_task_count": 5,
      "task_ids": [
        "zeros",
        "negative-set",
        "positive-set",
        "increasing-intervals",
        "decreasing-interval"
      ],
      "solution": "Written domain is exactly[-6,8]; graph grid/axes beyond it do not extendf. Letalpha<beta<gamma be the three actual zero abscissae, visually about-4,0.6,3; letp andq be the broad local maximum/minimum abscissae, visually about-2,2. Ordering:-6<alpha<p<beta<q<gamma<8. Complete zero set{alpha,beta,gamma}. Strict negative set[-6,alpha) union(beta,gamma), since the curve starts below0, crosses upward atalpha, downward atbeta and upward atgamma. Strict positive set(alpha,beta) union(gamma,8]. Each zero is excluded from either strict-sign set; finite endpoints are included in the sign matching their nonzero ordinate. Maximal increasing intervals[-6,p] and[q,8]; maximal decreasing interval[p,q]. Closed turning endpoints are admissible in strict monotonic intervals because for any two distinct ordered abscissae on one branch the function values are strictly ordered; derivative0 at a turning endpoint is not a failure of interval monotonicity. Numerically these intervals are about[-6,-2],[2,8] and[-2,2], not exact tick-labelled source givens. Full range of the displayed continuous graph is[f(-6),f(8)], approximately[-1.5,3]. Proof: first branch rises fromleft ordinate about-1.5 tolocal maximum about2; middle branch falls only toabout-1; last branch rises toabout3. Thus the local low point is above theleft endpoint and local high point below theright endpoint. All three branches stay within the global endpoint values; continuity on the first/last branches supplies every ordinate between the global min andmax. The range is not[-1,2]. Exact formula/crossing coordinates cannot be recovered from the pixels and are not fabricated."
    }
